<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proofs</h2>

<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Oracle Characterization of Margin-Adaptive Regularization ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">For mathematical representation in this proof, we adopt the notation <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y,y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y,y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are responses and <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary preference indicator such that <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\succ y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≻</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\succ y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}\succ y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≻</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}\succ y</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>). The introduction of <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> allows us to flexibly model preference directions using the BTL model, where <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}[d|x,y,y^{\prime}]=\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}[d|x,y,y^{\prime}]=\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We now analyze the expected loss for any triplet <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{low}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{low}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The analysis begins by taking the expectation of the sample-level loss over the optimal preference distribution <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This expectation simplifies to a new cross-entropy objective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The derivation proceeds as follows:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}\left[d\cdot w(h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+(1-d)\cdot w(-h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})|x,y,y^{\prime}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}\left[d\cdot w(h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+(1-d)\cdot w(-h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})|x,y,y^{\prime}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\left[\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})w(h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})w(-h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\left[\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})w(h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})w(-h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+\sigma(-c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+\sigma(-c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The second equality holds by BTL where <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}[d|x,y,y^{\prime}]=\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}[d|x,y,y^{\prime}]=\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The last equality is satisfied by the construction of <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h)</annotation></semantics></math>. This final form is the cross-entropy loss between the policy’s distribution, <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a margin-aware target distribution, <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}_{\phi^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}_{\phi^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with logits defined by <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the total loss, <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, is the expectation of these non-negative, instance-level cross-entropy losses over the dataset, the global minimum is achieved when the loss for each instance is minimized simultaneously. This occurs if and only if the distributions are identical for each instance, which requires their logits to be equal. Therefore, the optimal solution satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta h_{\theta^{*}}=c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta h_{\theta^{*}}=c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.1 Oracle Characterization of Margin-Adaptive Regularization ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">As we did for the proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Oracle Characterization of Margin-Adaptive Regularization ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we adopt the notation <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality, we assume <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi^{*}}\equiv h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y,y^{\prime})=r_{\phi^{*}}(x,y)-r_{\phi^{*}}(x,y^{\prime})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi^{*}}\equiv h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y,y^{\prime})=r_{\phi^{*}}(x,y)-r_{\phi^{*}}(x,y^{\prime})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> by swapping <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if necessary. So the weighting function is</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h_{\phi^{*}})=\frac{\sigma(c(h_{\phi^{*}})\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})}{\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})}\quad\text{and}\quad w(-h_{\phi^{*}})=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h_{\phi^{*}})=\frac{\sigma(c(h_{\phi^{*}})\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})}{\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})}\quad\text{and}\quad w(-h_{\phi^{*}})=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">We derive the expected loss for a triplet <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{high}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,y^{\prime},d)\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{high}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}[d|x,y,y^{\prime}]=\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{d\sim P_{\phi^{*}}}[d|x,y,y^{\prime}]=\sigma(h_{\phi^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math> from BTL model:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\log\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Now, we take derivatives of Eq. (<a href="#A2.E6" title="In Proof of Proposition . ‣ Appendix B Proofs ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) with respect to <math id="A2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta h_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta h_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, which results in</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})}{\partial\beta h_{\theta}}=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})}{\partial\beta h_{\theta}}=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">At the optimum <math id="A2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(c,\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\equiv\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})}{\partial\beta h_{\theta}}\bigg|_{\theta=\theta^{*}}=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\right]=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(c,\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\equiv\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\pi_{\theta},\phi^{*};x,y,y^{\prime})}{\partial\beta h_{\theta}}\bigg|_{\theta=\theta^{*}}=-\left[\sigma(c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\right]=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">We denote <math id="A2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\equiv c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\equiv c(|h_{\phi^{*}}|)</annotation></semantics></math> for notation simplicity. By the implicit function theorem, <math id="A2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}{\partial c}=-\frac{\partial F/\partial c}{\partial F/\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}{\partial c}=-\frac{\partial F/\partial c}{\partial F/\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Compute the partial derivatives:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial F}{\partial c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial F}{\partial c}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=h_{\phi^{*}}\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=h_{\phi^{*}}\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial F}{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial F}{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\cdot[-\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})]-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\cdot[\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\cdot[-\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})]-\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\cdot[\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">Thus:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}{\partial c}=-\frac{h_{\phi^{*}}\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})}{-\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\right]}=\frac{h_{\phi^{*}}\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})}{\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}{\partial c}=-\frac{h_{\phi^{*}}\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})}{-\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\right]}=\frac{h_{\phi^{*}}\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})}{\sigma(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta^{*}})\left[\sigma(c\cdot h_{\phi^{*}})+\sigma(-h_{\phi^{*}})\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.7.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi^{*}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi^{*}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and all sigmoid terms are strictly positive, the numerator and denominator are positive, so <math id="A2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}{\partial c}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\beta h_{\theta^{*}}}{\partial c}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="A2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})}{\partial c}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial(\beta h_{\theta^{*}})}{\partial c}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, proving the proposition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h)</annotation></semantics></math> be the weight function defined in Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Margin Adaptive Weight ‣ 3 Margin Adaptive Direct Preference Optimization (MADPO) ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The function is differentiable almost everywhere, and its derivative, <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math>, is uniformly bounded. That is, there exists a constant <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{w}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{w}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> where the derivative is defined:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|w^{\prime}(h)|\leq L_{w}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w^{\prime}(h)|\leq L_{w}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the purpose of this analysis, we can simplify the expression by fixing the hyperparameters to representative values. Hyperparameters’ specific value does not affect the following stability analysis, so for simplicity we let <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, along with <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\max}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\max}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By construction, the weight function <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous everywhere and differentiable almost everywhere. The only non differentiable points occur at <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0</annotation></semantics></math> due to the absolute value in the coefficient function <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and at <math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=-\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=-\tau</annotation></semantics></math> due to the piecewise definition.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1: For <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Let <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(h)=c(h)\cdot h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(h)=c(h)\cdot h</annotation></semantics></math>. The weight function and its derivative are:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h)=\frac{\sigma(k(h))}{\sigma(h)},\quad w^{\prime}(h)=\frac{\sigma^{\prime}(k(h))k^{\prime}(h)\sigma(h)-\sigma(k(h))\sigma^{\prime}(h)}{\sigma^{2}(h)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h)=\frac{\sigma(k(h))}{\sigma(h)},\quad w^{\prime}(h)=\frac{\sigma^{\prime}(k(h))k^{\prime}(h)\sigma(h)-\sigma(k(h))\sigma^{\prime}(h)}{\sigma^{2}(h)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The sigmoid function <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and its derivative <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> are universally bounded (by 1 and 1/4, respectively). For <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the denominator <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded away from zero. Therefore, to show that <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded, we only need to show that <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By the product rule, <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}(h)=c(h)+c^{\prime}(h)h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}(h)=c(h)+c^{\prime}(h)h</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded between <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[c_{\min},c_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[c_{\min},c_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The term <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}(h)h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}(h)h</annotation></semantics></math> involves the derivative of the coefficient function, which contains an exponential decay term that dominates the linear growth of <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}(h)h\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}(h)h\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous on <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> and has finite limits at its boundaries (<math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to 0^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to 0^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>), it is bounded on this interval. Thus, <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is also bounded for <math id="A2.I1.i1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2: For <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in(-\tau,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in(-\tau,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">On this bounded open interval, the derivative <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous function. As established in our analysis of the boundaries, the one-sided limits of <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to-\tau^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to-\tau^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and as <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to 0^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to 0^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are both finite. A function that is continuous on a bounded open interval and has finite limits at its endpoints is necessarily bounded. Thus, <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded on this interval.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3: For <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h&lt;-\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&lt;-\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">In this region, <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, its derivative is <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is trivially bounded.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the derivative <math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded on all three regions that cover its domain, we conclude that it is uniformly bounded.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The weight function <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Margin Adaptive Weight ‣ 3 Margin Adaptive Direct Preference Optimization (MADPO) ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous. That is, for any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h,h^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h,h^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following inequality holds:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|w(h)-w(h^{\prime})|\leq L_{w}|h-h^{\prime}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w(h)-w(h^{\prime})|\leq L_{w}|h-h^{\prime}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof relies on Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ Appendix B Proofs ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which establishes that the derivative of the weight function is bounded almost everywhere, i.e., <math id="A2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|w^{\prime}(t)|\leq L_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w^{\prime}(t)|\leq L_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. A function with a bounded derivative (a.e.) is absolutely continuous, which is the required condition to apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus for Lebesgue Integrals.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any two points <math id="A2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, the theorem states:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(b)-w(a)=\int_{a}^{b}w^{\prime}(t)dt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(b)-w(a)=\int_{a}^{b}w^{\prime}(t)dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">We can now take the absolute value of both sides and apply the bound from our lemma:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w(b)-w(a)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w(b)-w(a)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left|\int_{a}^{b}w^{\prime}(t)dt\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left|\int_{a}^{b}w^{\prime}(t)dt\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\int_{a}^{b}|w^{\prime}(t)|dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\int_{a}^{b}|w^{\prime}(t)|dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="A2.Ex41.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(Triangle inequality for integrals)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\int_{a}^{b}L_{w}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\int_{a}^{b}L_{w}dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="A2.Ex42.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(By Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ Appendix B Proofs ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <math id="A2.Ex42.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|w^{\prime}(t)|\leq L_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w^{\prime}(t)|\leq L_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=L_{w}(b-a)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=L_{w}(b-a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since this holds for any <math id="A2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, we can generalize to <math id="A2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|w(h)-w(h^{\prime})|\leq L_{w}|h-h^{\prime}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w(h)-w(h^{\prime})|\leq L_{w}|h-h^{\prime}|</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h,h^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h,h^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the definition of <math id="A2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6. ‣ 4.2 Lipschitz Continuity and Robustness to Reward Estimation Error ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof proceeds in two main parts. First, we establish that the margin function <math id="A2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz continuous with respect to its parameters <math id="A2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, we leverage this result to show that the full loss function, <math id="A2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi;x,y_{w},y_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi;x,y_{w},y_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, is also Lipschitz continuous, which allows us to invoke the final result from prior work.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 1: Lipschitz Continuity of the Margin Function (<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with the definition of the margin function: <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi}(x,y_{w},y_{l})=r_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-r_{\phi}(x,y_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi}(x,y_{w},y_{l})=r_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-r_{\phi}(x,y_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>. By the Mean Value Theorem, there exists a <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> on the line segment between <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})-h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})=\left(\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{w})-\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{l})\right)^{\top}(\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})-h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})=\left(\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{w})-\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{l})\right)^{\top}(\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Taking the absolute value and applying the generalized Cauchy-Schwarz inequality with the semi-norm <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}</annotation></semantics></math> gives:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|h_{\hat{\phi}}-h_{\phi^{*}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|h_{\hat{\phi}}-h_{\phi^{*}}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\left\|\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{w})-\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{l})\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I)^{-1}}\cdot\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\left\|\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{w})-\nabla_{\phi}r_{\bar{\phi}}(x,y_{l})\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I)^{-1}}\cdot\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Under Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Assumption 4.4. ‣ 4.2 Lipschitz Continuity and Robustness to Reward Estimation Error ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, the gradient of the reward function is bounded. This implies that the term involving the gradients is also bounded by some constant, which we will call <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the margin function is <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous with respect to <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})-h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})|\leq L_{\phi}\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})-h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})|\leq L_{\phi}\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Part 2: Lipschitz Continuity of the Full Loss Function (<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we analyze the full loss function, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi;x,y_{w},y_{l})=-w(h_{\phi}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi;x,y_{w},y_{l})=-w(h_{\phi}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="A2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi^{*};x,y_{w},y_{l})-\mathcal{L}(\theta,\hat{\phi};x,y_{w},y_{l})\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi^{*};x,y_{w},y_{l})-\mathcal{L}(\theta,\hat{\phi};x,y_{w},y_{l})\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex47.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\left|-\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{w},y_{l})\right|\cdot\left|w(h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))-w(h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left|-\log\sigma(\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{w},y_{l})\right|\cdot\left|w(h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))-w(h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq L_{\theta}\cdot\left|w(h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))-w(h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq L_{\theta}\cdot\left|w(h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))-w(h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l}))\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq L_{\theta}\cdot L_{w}\cdot|h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})-h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq L_{\theta}\cdot L_{w}\cdot|h_{\phi^{*}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})-h_{\hat{\phi}}(x,y_{w},y_{l})|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq L_{\theta}\cdot L_{w}\cdot L_{\phi}\cdot\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq L_{\theta}\cdot L_{w}\cdot L_{\phi}\cdot\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The second inequality holds by Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Assumption 4.5. ‣ 4.2 Lipschitz Continuity and Robustness to Reward Estimation Error ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>. The third inequality holds by Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ Appendix B Proofs ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The last inequality holds by Part 1 of this proof. By defining the final Lipschitz constant as <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=L_{\theta}L_{w}L_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=L_{\theta}L_{w}L_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have shown that our loss function is L-Lipschitz continuous:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi^{*};x,y_{w},y_{l})-\mathcal{L}(\theta,\hat{\phi};x,y_{w},y_{l})\right|\leq L\cdot\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi^{*};x,y_{w},y_{l})-\mathcal{L}(\theta,\hat{\phi};x,y_{w},y_{l})\right|\leq L\cdot\|\hat{\phi}-\phi^{*}\|_{\Sigma_{\phi}+\kappa I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">With this result established, the final statistical bound on the estimation error follows directly by invoking the general framework for two-step estimators, such as Theorem 4.2 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al. [2024]</a></cite>. This completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7. ‣ 4.3 Smoothness &amp; Curvature: MADPO vs. DPO ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For readability in this proof, we suppress the explicit dependence on the sample <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{w},y_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{w},y_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> and parameters <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\theta,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\theta,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> for functions like <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, unless required for clarity. The proof for both claims relies on the fact that the weight function, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h_{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h_{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>, is uniformly bounded. From its construction in Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.1 Margin Adaptive Weight ‣ 3 Margin Adaptive Direct Preference Optimization (MADPO) ‣ Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, there exists a constant <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|w(h_{\phi})|\leq w_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w(h_{\phi})|\leq w_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> for all inputs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bounded Gradient:</span> The first derivative of the loss is given by:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial h_{\theta}}=-w(h_{\phi})\cdot\beta\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial h_{\theta}}=-w(h_{\phi})\cdot\beta\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This expression is a product of the weight function, a constant <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and the sigmoid function, all of which are bounded. Therefore, their product is uniformly bounded.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bounded Hessian:</span> The second derivative of the loss is given by:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{L}}{\partial h_{\theta}^{2}}=w(h_{\phi})\cdot\beta^{2}\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{L}}{\partial h_{\theta}^{2}}=w(h_{\phi})\cdot\beta^{2}\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta})\sigma(\beta h_{\theta}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is a product of the bounded weight function, a constant <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the derivative of the sigmoid function, <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(\cdot)=\sigma(\cdot)\sigma(-\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(\cdot)=\sigma(\cdot)\sigma(-\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is famously bounded by 1/4. Therefore, the second derivative is also uniformly bounded.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent research in preference alignment has sought to address the limitations of DPO’s fixed temperature, which can lead to overfitting on easy, high-margin pairs. Prominent approaches such as IPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azar et al., 2024</a>]</cite> and <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>]</cite> tackle this problem with mechanisms that are applied at a coarse granularity. IPO proposes a uniform target margin for all samples, while <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-DPO’s strategies operate at the batch level.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While an improvement, the batch-level mechanisms of <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-DPO have several notable drawbacks. First, its adaptation is a coarse approximation of the instance-level ideal. A single training batch can easily contain a mix of high- and low-margin pairs, yet the <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-batch method applies a single, compromised temperature to all of them. Second, the linear adaptation rule, <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\text{batch}}=\beta_{0}(1+m\cdot(\bar{h}_{\theta}-h_{0}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>batch</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\text{batch}}=\beta_{0}(1+m\cdot(\bar{h}_{\theta}-h_{0}))</annotation></semantics></math>, can be unstable; for difficult batches where the average margin <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is negative, the resulting temperature <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\text{batch}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>batch</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\text{batch}}</annotation></semantics></math> can also become negative, leading to a divergent objective that actively learns to prefer the dispreferred response. Finally, the batch-dependent temperature complicates hyperparameter selection, as the absence of a fixed <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> makes it difficult to perform reliable cross-validation to find the optimal tuning parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our work, MADPO, is distinct in that it provides a fully instance-level and data-preserving solution that avoids these issues. By applying a continuous, adaptive weight to each training sample based on its unique reward margin, our method can granularly control the learning signal. This allows it to be aggressive on hard pairs and conservative on easy ones within the same batch, providing a more flexible and stable approach to preference alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the methods discussed above, other recent works have extended the DPO framework in various directions. For instance, methods like SimPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>]</cite> and <math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>]</cite> focus on simplifying the objective by removing the need for an explicit reference policy. Another line of work has explored reweighting preference data. Omni-DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>]</cite> dynamically weights pairs based on both their inherent quality and the model’s current learning state, while WPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>]</cite> reweights off-policy preference data to more closely resemble the on-policy distribution. While these methods also use a reweighting scheme, their motivation is distinct from that of MADPO. Whereas these approaches weight data based on the policy’s dynamic state or distributional properties, MADPO’s weighting is based on a static, external signal of sample difficulty derived from the reward margin, <math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is not to correct for distributional shift, but to granularly control the regularization strength for each individual sample based on its intrinsic difficulty.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
